{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WE3eftuD4FjuHirAL83MKR","type":"tool_result","content":"86-They can be roughly divided into enumerative [25,26,33], ﬁxed-parameter [7,12,19,22] and combinatorial branch-and-bound\n87-algorithms [14,20], and ﬁnally mixed-integer programming (MIP) models [3,13,17,20,23,32]. While a combinatorial branch-\n88:and-bound method appears to be the currently fastest and most robust method for small and moderately sized graphs in\n89-practice, we aim at revealing the limitations and structural weaknesses of present MIP formulations and at improving their\n90-competitiveness. In this respect, the contribution of this paper is threefold.\n91-    First, we derive two MIP formulations that represent the current state-of-the-art. Then, we reveal one essential reason\n92-why these formulations are of poor utility in solving larger or more diﬃcult instances to optimality. More precisely, we\n93-show that, for any given graph, the lower bound on its pathwidth obtained when solving their linear programming (LP)\n94-relaxations, i.e., the linear program that arises when neglecting any integrality requirements on the variables, is zero. Second,\n--\n545-20 Tree instances from the VSPLIB [17]. Then, there are 20 graphs whose bidirected arcs represent the non-zero off-diagonal\n546-entries of instances from the Harwell–Boeing sparse matrix collection (denoted HB) [18], and another set of 84 instances\n547:with only 16–24 vertices called Small that were introduced in [30].\n548-   All LPs and MIPs were solved using version 12.6 of CPLEX.3 For maximum fairness and to reﬂect the effects of each\n549-formulation as purely as possible, all its internal cutting plane algorithms, heuristics, and presolve routines were disabled.\n550-Each run was executed single-threadedly on a Debian Linux machine with an Intel Core i7-3770T processor running at\n551-2.5 GHz and with 32 GB RAM.\n552-\n553-6.1. Results\n554-\n555-    There is a table for each considered benchmark subset. Table 1 gives the results for the set TWLib, Table 2 for the set HB,\n556:Table 3 for the Tree and Grid graphs, and ﬁnally Tables 4 and 5 for the Small instances. In case of the trees, we restricted\n557-the displayed results to four instances with indices 1, 6, and 11 as these are representative for all the graphs with indices\n558-1–5, 6–10 and 11–15, respectively. Each of the tables reports the following information: The instance name, the number of\n559-vertices n, the number of directed arcs m and the pathwidth of the graph. Then, in column ﬁve, the lower bound obtained\n560-with LP L is given. Columns 6–8 display the time required for solving the respective LP relaxations. The time speciﬁed for\n561-LP L is the total time for iteratively solving LPs and adding three-dicycle-inequalities until a solution is found where none of\n562-these are violated. The last three columns give the results for solving MIP L , MIP P , and MIP S . If an instance could be solved\n--\n626-the other instances from the HB set reﬂect sparse graphs (between 2 and 25% of the possible arcs exist). Often, they\n627-have a (possibly slightly incomplete) canonical bidirected Hamiltonian path, and a few shorter ‘shortcutting’ subpaths that\n628:represent a banded matrix structure. Across these, and also the Small instances, the lower bound quality is about 50% on\n629-average, but can be considerably worse (e.g., 30% for can___96) or much better (even 99% for dwt___66). In case of the\n630-TWLib instances, the picture is similar and, apart from the queen-graphs, they also have a similar density between 8 and\n631-25%. For the queen-graphs with a density between about 30% and 54%, the quality of the lower bound drops considerably\n632-– with an increasing number of vertices and decreasing density. However, as one can also see from the results, in general,\n633-very low or high densities and the (ir)regularity of vertex degree patterns can lead to both, better or worse, results.\n634-\n--\n644-\n645-         Table 4\n646:         Results for the Small instance test bed (part one).\n647-\n648-           Instance         n         m         pw        LB             Time [s]                           Time [s] or LB\n649-                                                          LP L           LP L       LP P        LP S        MIP L        MIP P     MIP S\n650-           p17_16_24         16       48        4         2.0833         0.01       0.04        0.02        1.79         13.66     0.64\n651-           p18_16_21         16       42        3         1.7273         0.01       0.02        0.01        0.53         4.73      0.41\n652-           p19_16_19         16       38        3         1.5081         0.01       0.02        0.01        0.46         22.29     0.54\n--\n704-\n705-6.1.3. How do MIP P , MIP S , and MIP L perform with a sophisticated solver?\n706:    Most (73 of 84) Small instances could be solved easily with all formulations. Also in case of the other instances, optimum\n707-or near-optimal solutions were typically found quickly with all formulations, but often optimality cannot be proven within\n708-the time limit. Concerning the number of solved instances and derived lower bounds after ten minutes of computation time,\n709-MIP P is inferior to at least one of the other two models except for seven instances in total. MIP S solves a few instances more\n710-than MIP L within the time limit. On the other hand, for many unsolved instances, MIP L provides a better lower bound than\n711-MIP S when the timeout occurs. However, further investigations revealed that the branch-and-bound scheme of CPLEX is able\n712-to improve the global lower bound much better when solving MIP S than when solving MIP L . More precisely, MIP L starts\n--\n719-\n815:[17] A. Duarte, L.F. Escudero, R. Martí, N. Mladenovic, J.J. Pantrigo, J. Sánchez-Oro, Variable neighborhood search for the vertex separation problem, C