{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011LTrQjCNh6N1wRdDy3qf7D","type":"tool_result","content":"=== yanasse_1997a_transformation_pattern_sequencing_wood.pdf\nA TRANSFORMATION FOR SOLVING A PATTERN SEQUENCING PROBLEM IN\n                   THE WOOD CUT INDUSTRY\n                                Horacio Hideki Yanasse\n               Associate Laboratory for Computing and Applied Mathematics\n                            National Space Research Institute\n                                           Abstract\nConsider a wood cutting setting where different panels have to be cut from large boards. Each\ncut panel size is put into a stack which remains opened until the last panel of that size is cut.\nThe minimization of open stack problem (MOSP) is the problem of sequencing the cutting\npatterns in order to minimize the maximum number of open stacks during the production run.\nIt is shown that an optimal solution to any MOSP can be obtained from an optimal solution of\na corresponding MOSP where all patterns have at most two different panel types.\nKeywords: cutting stock, pattern sequencing, pattern allocation, graph traversing\n                                        Resumo\nConsidere uma indústria de corte de madeira onde painéis de tamanhos diferentes são\ncortados de grandes chapas. Cada tamanho diferente de painel cortado abre uma pilha nova\nque permanece aberta até que o último painel daquele tamanho seja cortado. O problema de\nminimização de pilhas abertas (PMPA) consiste em determinar um sequenciamento dos\npadrões de corte de modo a minimizar o número máximo de pilhas abertas durante o processo\nde corte. Mostra-se que uma solução ótima para qualquer PMPA pode ser obtido de uma\nsolução ótima de um outro PMPA correspondente onde todos os padrões tem no máximo dois\ntamanhos diferentes de painéis.\nPalavras-chave: corte de estoque, sequenciamento de padrões, percorrimento em grafos\n\f1. INTRODUCTION\n        Consider, for instance, a wood cutting setting where different panel sizes are to be cut\n=== chu_2011_phd_thesis_improving_combinatorial_optimization.pdf\nMinerva Access is the Institutional Repository of The University of Melbourne\nAuthor/s:\nChu, Geoﬀrey G.\nTitle:\nImproving combinatorial optimization\nDate:\n\nCitation:\nChu, G. G. (). Improving combinatorial optimization. PhD thesis, Engineering,\nComputer Science and Software Engineering, The University of Melbourne.\nPersistent Link:\nhttps://hdl.handle.net//\nTerms and Conditions:\nTerms and Conditions: Copyright in works deposited in Minerva Access is retained\nby the copyright owner. The work may not be altered without permission from the\ncopyright owner. Readers may only download, print and save electronic copies of whole\nworks for their own personal non-commercial use. Any use that exceeds these limits\nrequires permission from the copyright owner. Attribution is essential when quoting or\nparaphrasing from these works.\n\f   Improving Combinatorial Optimization\n                           by\n                   Geoffrey G Chu\n             Submitted in total fulfillment\n                  of the requirements\n          of the degree of Doctor of Philosophy\n=== fink_2012_phd_thesis_mosp_novas_contribuicoes.pdf\nO problema de minimização de pilhas abertas –\n            novas contribuições\n                  Claudia Fink\n=== smith*\n=== 2005-Smith-Gent-Constraint-Modelling-Challenge-2005-Proceedings-and-Report.pdf\n                                                                                                    Constraint Modelling Challenge 2005\n                                    Constraint Modelling Challenge 2005\n                              In conjunction with The Fifth Workshop on\n                          Modelling and Solving Problems with Constraints\n                        Held at IJCAI 2005, Edinburgh, Scotland, 31 July, 2005\n                                Barbara M. Smith and Ia